Package: mos-rauc
Version: @VERSION@
Architecture: @ARCH@
Maintainer: mos build <mos@example.invalid>
Section: admin
Priority: optional
Depends: default-dbus-system-bus | dbus-system-bus, mount, ${shlibs:Depends}
Description: mos update client (RAUC)
 The A/B update client: the RAUC binary built from upstream source with
 -Dnetwork=false -Dstreaming=false, its systemd unit, the helper the D-Bus
 service file Execs, the bus policy for de.pengutronix.rauc and the activation
 file itself. All five are on the activation path -- Debian splits them across
 two packages, and a rauc with no service files answers "the name
 de.pengutronix.rauc was not provided by any .service files" while a health
 gate reports green.
 .
 GLib, json-glib, OpenSSL, libfdisk and libc are what the binary LINKS, so
 dpkg-shlibdeps writes them into Depends from the ELF itself, at the target
 architecture, and none of them is named above: a hand-written soname package
 is a version that stops matching the first time the builder image moves.
 .
 A D-Bus system bus is not visible to dpkg-shlibdeps, because rauc talks to the
 bus through GDBus in libgio and links no libdbus-1 at all -- so the one
 relationship that makes this package's own D-Bus activation file mean anything
 has to be written out.
 .
 mount is not visible to it either. Every install mounts the bundle and then
 unmounts it, and rauc does that by exec'ing the mount and umount PROGRAMS by
 name rather than through libmount; the mount package is what provides both.
 .
 The unit ships DISABLED: no multi-user.target.wants symlink is in this
 payload and nothing enables it. The daemon is reached through D-Bus
 activation, which is what de.pengutronix.rauc.service is for.
 .
 The board's system.conf and bootloader configuration are deliberately not
 here. Both are board facts and belong to the board package, which is also
 where grub-editenv is installed, gated on the board's RAUC_BOOTLOADER.
